   "Solve the corresponding equation to find the critical values",
   "Sketch the parabola to identify which region is above/below zero",
   "For < 0: between the roots. For > 0: outside the roots"
  ],
  "title": "How to Solve Quadratic Inequalities and Shade Regions",
  "content": "<p><strong>Quadratic inequalities</strong> involve \\(x^2\\) terms. To solve, first find where the quadratic equals zero (the critical values), then use a sketch to determine which region satisfies the inequality.</p><p><strong>Method:</strong> 1) Rearrange so one side is 0. 2) Factorise or use the formula to find roots. 3) Sketch the parabola (U-shape for positive \\(x^2\\)). 4) Read off the solution: for \\(< 0\\), the values are between the roots; for \\(> 0\\), the values are outside the roots.</p><p><strong>Graphical inequalities:</strong> Draw boundary lines. Use solid lines for \\(\\le/\\ge\\) and dashed for \\(</>\\). Test a point (often the origin) to decide which side to shade.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(x^2 - 5x + 6 < 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Factorise: \\((x-2)(x-3) < 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Roots at \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Parabola is U-shaped (positive \\(x^2\\)), so it's below zero <em>between</em> the roots</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(2 < x < 3\\)</p>"
 },
